Human blood albumin is a plasma-derived therapeutic protein that helps maintain blood volume and oncotic pressure while transporting hormones, fatty acids and other substances throughout the body. It is widely used in clinical settings to manage selected cases of hypovolemia, severe burns, shock and hypoalbuminemia. Its role in stabilizing fluid balance makes it an important product in hospital-based critical care and specialized treatment.

Demand is supported by the growing need for plasma-derived therapies, expanding healthcare infrastructure and increased use of albumin in pharmaceutical formulations. However, dependence on human plasma collection, complex fractionation processes, high manufacturing costs and stringent quality requirements continue to influence production capacity and market access.

